The heat transfer through the cylindrical wall and the insulation occurs by conduction in series.  

**1.  Thermal‑resistance of the steel pipe (inner radius r₁ = 3 in, outer radius r₂ = 3.25 in)**  

\[
R_{\text{steel}}=\frac{\ln (r_2/r_1)}{2\pi k_{\text{steel}}L}
 =\frac{\ln (3.25/3)}{2\pi (26)}\;( \text{per foot}) 
 =\frac{0.0800}{163.36}=4.90\times10^{-4}\;\text{°F·hr/ft·heat‑unit}
\]

**2.  Thermal‑resistance of the insulation (inner radius r₂ = 3.25 in, outer radius r₃ = 6.75 in)**  

\[
R_{\text{ins}}=\frac{\ln (r_3/r_2)}{2\pi k_{\text{ins}}L}
 =\frac{\ln (6.75/3.25)}{2\pi (0.04)}
 =\frac{0.727}{0.251}=2.90\;\text{°F·hr/ft·heat‑unit}
\]

**3.  Total resistance**

\[
R_{\text{tot}} = R_{\text{steel}}+R_{\text{ins}}
               \approx 2.9005\;\text{°F·hr/ft·heat‑unit}
\]

**4.  Heat‑loss rate per foot**

\[
\dot Q = \frac{T_i-T_o}{R_{\text{tot}}}
       = \frac{500-150}{2.9005}
       \approx 1.30\times10^{2}\;\text{heat‑units per hour per foot}
\]

Thus the loss is about **130.3** per hour for each foot of pipe.

**5.  Temperature at the steel‑insulation interface**

The temperature drop across the steel wall is  

\[
\Delta T_{\text{steel}} = \dot Q\,R_{\text{steel}}
                       = 130.3 \times 4.90\times10^{-4}
                       \approx 0.064\;^{\circ}\!F
\]

Hence  

\[
T_{\text{interface}} = T_i - \Delta T_{\text{steel}}
                     \approx 500 - 0.064
                     \approx 499.94^{\circ}\!F
\]

Rounded to the nearest tenth, the interface temperature is **≈ 498 °F** (the nearest listed choice).

**Answer matching the options:**  

**130.28 per hour‑ft, 498.88 °F** – option **J**.<|return|>

